 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a socket per connected browser and publishes each committed revision as it lands. The project runs the sockets itself; no realtime vendor is involved. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required, and socket delivery meets the fifteen-second freshness goal under normal connectivity.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable revision history so a browser can catch up after a disconnect; delivery manages socket sessions and reliable publication of each committed revision; browsers reconnect and catch up from a revision cursor. Five-second HTTPS polling was considered and rejected: the simplest stateless operation, but it entails repeated reads, a few seconds of lag, and a polling loop in every browser. A hosted realtime service was considered and rejected: the quickest route to low-latency updates with fewer connection operations, but it adds recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets accept connection-capacity and reconnect operations as the cost of fast updates with no realtime vendor dependency.
 
